Why This Is Asked
- Validates per-tenant auditable trails without impacting throughput
- Ensures tamper evidence via signatures and secure storage
- Checks ability to query across tenants with predictable retention

Key Concepts
- Append-only logs, tenant isolation, data integrity, retention strategy
- Tamper detection using MACs or signatures, and secure storage backends
- API design for write/read with minimal surface area and clear semantics

Code Example
```javascript
class AuditLog {
  constructor(storage, signer) {
    this.storage = storage; // per-tenant append-only store
    this.signer = signer;   // cryptographic signing service
  }

  async write(tenant, event) {
    const entry = {
      timestamp: Date.now(),
      tenant,
      event,
      signature: await this.signer.sign(event)
    };
    return await this.storage.append(tenant, entry);
  }

  async query(tenant, since, limit) {
    const entries = await this.storage.read(tenant, since, limit);
    return entries.filter(entry => 
      this.signer.verify(entry.event, entry.signature)
    );
  }
}
```

## Why This Question Matters
This question tests your ability to handle late data gracefully without breaking at-least-once delivery guarantees. It probes your understanding of per-topic state management, routing decisions under delayed data conditions, and offset tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Per-topic watermarks for independent time-window tracking
- Late data routing based on watermark thresholds
- Offset tracking and message deduplication for at-least-once semantics
- Canary testing to validate both in-order and late-data paths

## Code Example
```javascript
class WatermarkManager {
  constructor(maxLagMs = 60000) {
    this.maxLagMs = maxLagMs;
    this.topicWatermarks = new Map();
    this.offsetStore = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(topic, partition, offset, timestamp) {
    const key = `${topic}-${partition}`;
    const currentWatermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key) || 0;

    if (timestamp > currentWatermark) {
      this.topicWatermarks.set(key, timestamp);
    }

    const watermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key);
    const lag = watermark - timestamp;

    this.offsetStore.set(key, offset);

    if (lag > this.maxLagMs) {
      return { route: 'late_events', topic, partition, offset };
    }

    return { route: 'main', topic, partition, offset };
  }
}
```

This implementation maintains per-topic watermarks, routes late messages appropriately, tracks offsets, and supports at-least-once delivery through committed offset storage.

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of cross-region consistency, exactly-once semantics, and failover handling—critical skills for building regulated audit systems that require high reliability and compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-region replication and exactly-once delivery guarantees
- Idempotent sinks with durable deduplication state
- Transactional outbox pattern for atomic offset commits
- Mapping local partition offsets to a globally ordered sequence
- Replay resilience and failover recovery strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: Exactly-once consumer with transactional outbox
async function processEvent(event, consumer, producer) {
  const globalId = `${event.region}||${event.partition}||${event.offset}`;

  // Check dedup store
  if (await dedupStore.exists(globalId)) {
    await consumer.commitOffsets([{ topic: event.topic, partition: event.partition, offset: event.offset + 1 }]);
    return;
  }

  // Transaction: write audit + commit offset
  await producer.beginTransaction();
  try {
    await writeToAuditTable(globalId, event.payload);
    await dedupStore.set(globalId, { ttl: 86400 });
    await producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction([{ topic: event.topic, partition: event.partition, offset: event.offset + 1 }], consumerGroupId);
    await producer.commitTransaction();
  } catch (err) {
    await producer.abortTransaction();
    throw err;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design resilient cross-region messaging systems with strong consistency guarantees while maintaining multi-tenant isolation and data residency requirements.

## Key Concepts

- KCNA: Kafka-compatible distributed messaging with native multi-tenancy
- Active-active cross-region replication with bidirectional log streaming
- Per-tenant data residency through isolated partitioning
- Hybrid ordering: local sequences + lightweight global sequencer
- Exactly-once semantics through idempotent producers and transactional writes
- Failover mechanisms with automatic leader election and log replay

## Code Example

```javascript
// KCNA publisher with idempotency and cross-region coordination
class KCNAPublisher {
  constructor(tenantId, region, globalSequencer) {
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.region = region;
    this.sequencer = globalSequencer;
    this.dedupStore = new Map();
  }

  async publishEvent(event) {
    const messageId = `${this.tenantId}:${this.region}:${event.id}`;

    // Idempotency check
    if (this.dedupStore.has(messageId)) {
      return { status: 'duplicate', seq: null };
    }

    // Get global sequence for ordering
    const globalSeq = await this.sequencer.getNextSequence(
      this.tenantId, this.region
    );

    const enrichedEvent = {
      ...event,
      tenantId: this.tenantId,
      region: this.region,
      globalSequence: globalSeq,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    };

    // Write to local log with transaction
    await this.writeToLog(enrichedEvent);
    this.dedupStore.set(messageId, true);

    // Trigger cross-region replication
    await this.replicateToPeerRegion(enrichedEvent);

    return { status: 'success', seq: globalSeq };
  }

  async writeToLog(event) {
    // Transactional write to tenant-specific partition
    const partition = this.getPartition(event.tenantId);
    return partition.append(event, { transactional: true });
  }

  async replicateToPeerRegion(event) {
    // Async replication to peer region
    const peerRegion = this.region === 'A' ? 'B' : 'A';
    return this.replicator.send(peerRegion, event);
  }
}

// Global sequencer for cross-region ordering
class GlobalSequencer {
  constructor() {
    this.counters = new Map(); // tenantId -> sequence
  }

  async getNextSequence(tenantId, region) {
    const key = `${tenantId}`;
    const current = this.counters.get(key) || 0;
    const next = current + 1;
    this.counters.set(key, next);
    return next;
  }
}
```

